From: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: refuse an HFWD ring the queue cannot hold

airoha_qdma_init_hfwd_queues() derives the descriptor count from the size of
the memory-region the DTS reserves, then writes it into
HW_FWD_DESC_NUM_MASK, a 17-bit field.  A reservation larger than 131072
payloads wraps around the field, and the queue is then programmed with a
truncated descriptor count while the driver keeps handing the hardware the
full buffer: descriptors past the wrap are never used again, and the payload
area the hardware can address no longer matches the one the driver filled.

The current DTS files are well inside the limit (32 MiB / 2 KiB and 16 MiB /
1 KiB, 16384 descriptors each), so nothing is broken today; that is exactly
why the bound belongs here, next to the division that produces the number,
instead of being re-derived by hand whenever someone resizes the region.

Reject a count the field cannot express, and a zero count, which allocates a
zero-length descriptor array and leaves the queue with no descriptors at all.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
@@ -1690,6 +1690,12 @@
 		 * reserved memory size and the payload buffer size
 		 */
 		num_desc = div_u64(rmem->size, buf_size);
+		if (!num_desc || num_desc > HW_FWD_DESC_NUM_MASK) {
+			dev_err(eth->dev,
+				"%s needs %d descriptors, only 1-%lu fit\n",
+				name, num_desc, HW_FWD_DESC_NUM_MASK);
+			return -EINVAL;
+		}
 	} else {
 		size = buf_size * num_desc;
 		if (!dmam_alloc_coherent(eth->dev, size, &dma_addr,
